etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4191030\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: wsvn.com\nSLUG: trump-and-pakistan-say-iran-deal-could-be-signed-sunday-but-tehran-signals-more-time-is-needed\nTEXT: US President Trump and Pakistan suggest an Iran deal could be signed on Sunday, but Tehran signals more time is needed. The US has blockaded Iranian ports in response to the deal, which focuses on ending the war and does not discuss Iran\'s nuclear issue. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi posted on X that an agreement "has never been closer." Trump claimed significant progress in negotiations, hours after threatening to seize Iran\'s oil industry. The apparent breakthrough comes after a week of Iranian fire with the US and Israel, threatening to rupture the ceasefire.\n\nID: 4192128\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: geo.tv\nSLUG: 668679-iran-says-draft-us-deal-includes-oil-sanctions-waiver-nuclear-limits-and-asset-release\nTEXT: Iran has agreed to a deal with the US that includes waiving oil sanctions, releasing frozen assets and diluting enriched uranium on its soil. The deal covers a range of issues, including Tehran\'s nuclear programme and the reopening of the Strait of Hormuz. Under the draft, Iran would immediately reopen the Strait to all commercial vessels and the US would lift its naval blockade on Iranian ports. The US has agreed not to impose any new sanctions on Iran until a final agreement is reached, and all US and UN sanctions would be removed according to a mutually agreed timetable.\n\nID: 4192546\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: abc.net.au\nSLUG: 106715420\nTEXT: The US and Iran have agreed to end hostilities, with the Strait of Hormuz set to be reopened. The deal is seen as a major breakthrough in relations between the two countries, with Foreign Minister Penny Wong welcoming the agreement. US President Donald Trump claims victory for the peace deal, while British PM welcomes the move. However, nuclear issues are still to be discussed, with Iran warning that it will remain vigilant in case the agreement is breached. The deal includes Lebanon, and Trump has expressed support for "letting the oil flow".\n\nID: 4192378\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: channelnewsasia.com\nSLUG: iran-peace-deal-strait-hormuz-6182881\nTEXT: A draft peace deal between Iran and the US could reopen the Strait of Hormuz, end a US blockade on Iranian ports, and extend a ceasefire. The deal would allow ships to pass through the strait "toll free" and end a US naval blockade. Trump tweeted that ships should "start their engines... Let the oil flow!". However, Iranian negotiator Mohammad Baqer Qalibaf criticized the US for lacking "the will and ability to fulfill your commitments" after Israel\'s attack on Beirut, which targeted Iranian-backed Hezbollah militants.\n\nID: 4192141\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: the-star.co.ke\nSLUG: 2026-06-14-iran-has-not-decided-on-proposed-mou-with-us\nTEXT: Iran\'s Foreign Minister Seyed Abbas Araghchi said the signing of a proposed Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with the US could take place within the next few days. The MoU would include provisions to end the war on all fronts, including Lebanon, and require Israel to withdraw from occupied areas. Key issues would include reopening the Strait of Hormuz, lifting the US anti-Iran naval blockade, and releasing Iran\'s frozen assets.\n\nID: 4191956\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: tbsnews.net\nSLUG: iran-says-draft-us-deal-includes-oil-sanctions-waiver-nuclear-limits-and-asset-release-1462951\nTEXT: Iran has agreed to a draft deal with the US, covering issues such as nuclear work and oil sanctions. The final draft of the Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) includes a waiver on oil sanctions, allowing Iran to export its oil. The deal also addresses the reopening of the Strait of Hormuz and limits on nuclear activities. A senior Iranian official stated that a final deal will be discussed in 60 days following agreement by both sides.\n\nID: 4191586\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: thecitizen.co.tz\nSLUG: us-iran-inch-closer-to-deal-trump-says-sunday-but-timing-remains-unclear-5495732\nTEXT: Iran is inching closer to a deal, with US President Trump stating on Sunday that progress has been made. However, the timing remains unclear. According to Fars news agency, Tehran is still reviewing its political, legal and technical aspects of the framework agreement. Qatari negotiators visited Tehran on Sunday morning as part of efforts to finalize the deal, but a final decision has yet to be made.\n\nID: 4191772\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: nypost.com\nSLUG: iran-agrees-not-to-build-a-nuke-will-reopen-strait-of-hormuz-and-us-to-release-25b-in-assets-in-trump-peace-deal-tehran\nTEXT: US President Donald Trump and Pakistani Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif agreed on a deal to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, a crucial chokepoint for oil supplies. The deal will also result in the release of $25 billion in assets, as part of a Trump peace plan. The agreement was made on Saturday and will be signed on Sunday, with the Strait of Hormuz expected to reopen immediately.\n\nID: 4190744\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2364976\nTEXT: US President Trump stated that the US-Iran deal, which includes opening up the Hormuz Strait, will be signed on Sunday. According to Araghchi, Iran\'s enriched uranium can only be dealt with by diluting it inside the country. Trump previously said the US would remove and destroy the uranium, but now claims to "get the Nuclear Dust" and downblend it in either Iran or the US.\n\nID: 4190671\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: lokmattimes.com\nSLUG: us-iran-peace-deal-to-be-signed-today\nTEXT: US President Donald Trump announced that a peace deal with Iran is scheduled to be signed on Sunday, with the Strait of Hormuz reopening immediately afterward.\n\n\n',)
