etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4190743\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2364979\nTEXT: US President Trump has named James M. McDonald, a former federal prosecutor and financial regulator, to lead the powerful New York Federal Prosecutors Office. McDonald previously served as a financial regulator during Trump\'s first term and worked in the White House counsel\'s office under President George W. Bush. He is part of Trump\'s legal team handling the pending appeal of felony convictions related to hush money payments to adult film actor Stormy Daniels. McDonald\'s new role would give him oversight of terrorism, espionage, security fraud, and public corruption cases. He previously secured a favorable outcome for Indian billionaire Gautam Adani when the Trump administration Justice Department dropped a fraud and conspiracy case.\n\nID: 4192591\nCREATED: 2026-06-14\nDOMAIN: ibtimes.sg\nSLUG: who-james-m-mcdonald-what-know-about-trumps-pick-manhattans-top-federal-prosecutor-87932\nTEXT: James M. McDonald, a former federal prosecutor in the Southern District of New York and director of enforcement at the Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC) during Trump\'s first term, has been nominated as the next U.S. Attorney for the SDNY by President Donald Trump. If confirmed, McDonald will lead the influential Manhattan-based office and succeed Jay Clayton, whom Trump recently nominated to serve as director of national intelligence. McDonald previously served as a federal prosecutor in the SDNY and has represented high-profile clients, including Trump and Gautam Adani. His appointment is seen as a familiar face at the SDNY, which handles high-profile cases involving terrorism, espionage, and securities fraud.\n\n\n',)
